Subject: Fuse `x += s` String Accumulation Into The Accumulator

What this does
  In OP_add's string slow path, and in OP_add_loc, a store that follows the add
  and targets the very JSString being concatenated is recognised, the append is
  made into that string's own buffer, and the store is skipped.

Why we need it
  `x += s` compiles to a load of x, the right-hand side, an add and a store back
  to x. The load duplicates the reference, so at the add the accumulator is held
  by both x's storage and the operand stack, JS_ConcatString2's in-place extend
  requires refcount 1, and the whole accumulator is copied instead.

What it adds
  The slot is identified from the store instruction that follows -- the put_loc
  forms, or put_field/put_field_ic with the receiver under the operands -- and
  proven to be the other holder by pointer identity: with the slot holding a
  bit-identical JSValue and refcount == 1 + stack_ref, the only holders are that
  slot and this operand stack. Any third holder -- a second variable, an
  atom-table entry, a slice parent link, a Map key, a captured closure copy --
  makes it 3 and the generic path runs. Appends memcpy into the accumulator's own
  malloc slack, or realloc with half-again headroom capped at 64 KB, which turns
  repeated appends from O(n^2) into amortised O(n). The helper packs its result
  into its return value rather than taking an out-parameter, because an
  out-parameter costs JS_CallInternal a local whose address is taken.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
